ence of 100 genes", "transformation_chain_verbatim": ["prompt template selection", "combine the prompt with the cell sentence"], "model_visible_form_verbatim": "prompt containing a sequence of 100 genes", "carrier_family": "text_native_token_stream", "carrier_subtype": "serialized_biological_context_or_ordered_profile", "insertion_or_fusion_verbatim": "combine the prompt with the cell sentence", "evidence_quote": "generate a cell type label following a provided sequence of 100 genes", "section_heading": "3.2.1 Tasks", "supporting_figure_or_table": "Figure 6"}, {"route_id": "route_6e6e12bf204d", "route_label": "GPT-2 Small Cell2Sentence encoding", "lifecycle_phase": "pretraining", "task_or_configuration_verbatim": "Cell2Sentence transformation", "source_object_verbatim": "single-cell gene expression count matrix", "transformation_chain_verbatim": ["filter cells and genes", "quality control", "row-normalization to 10,000 transcript counts", "log-normalization", "rank-order transformation", "truncate to top 100 genes"], "model_visible_form_verbatim": "cell sentences", "carrier_family": "text_native_token_stream", "carrier_subtype": "serialized_biological_context_or_ordered_profile", "insertion_or_fusion_verbatim": "converted into cell sentences", "evidence_quote": "Figure 1: Overview of the Cell2Sentence framework. Input single-cell data, including metadata, are converted into cell sentences for LLM fine-tuning. Inference, via prompting, generates new cell sentences that can be converted back to gene expression space.", "section_heading": "1 Introduction", "supporting_figure_or_table": "Figure 2"}, {"route_id": "route_76a243c3cd9d", "route_label": "GPT-2 Small metadata-conditioned cell generation", "lifecycle_phase": "inference", "task_or_configuration_verbatim": "generate cells from text prompts", "source_object_verbatim": "natural language prompt containing cell type, tissue, or disease metadata", "transformation_chain_verbatim": ["prompt template selection", "merge prompt with the specified biological metadata"], "model_visible_form_verbatim": "text prompt", "carrier_family": "text_native_token_stream", "carrier_subtype": "structured_biological_prompt_or_task_scaffold", "insertion_or_fusion_verbatim": "formatted as prompts", "evidence_quote": "Model Input: “T-cell in multiple sclerosis”", "section_heading": null, "supporting_figure_or_table": "Figure 1"}, {"route_id": "route_95b1852fc5f4", "route_label": "GPT-2 Small metadata-annotated Cell2Sentence encoding", "lifecycle_phase": "pretraining", "task_or_configuration_verbatim": "Cell2Sentence transformation with biological metadata annotations", "source_object_verbatim": "single-cell data including metadata annotations", "transformation_chain_verbatim": ["filter cells and genes", "quality control", "row-normalization to 10,000 transcript counts", "log-normalization", "rank-order transformation", "attach biological metadata such as cell type, tissue, or disease", "truncate to top 100 genes"], "model_visible_form_verbatim": "cell sentences with biological metadata", "carrier_family": "text_native_token_stream", "carrier_subtype": "serialized_biological_context_or_ordered_profile", "insertion_or_fusion_verbatim": "converted into cell sentences", "evidence_quote": "Cell sentences may be annotated with biological metadata, such as cell type, tissue, or disease.", "section_heading": "2 Results", "supporting_figure_or_table": "Figure 2"}]}

Figure captions and native VLM descriptions:
[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 3, "caption": "Figure 1: Overview of the Cell2Sentence framework. Input single-cell data, including metadata, are converted into cell sentences for LLM fine-tuning. Inference, via prompting, generates new cell sentences that can be converted back to gene expression space.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a workflow schematic for single-cell data generation using Cell2Sentence and LLM fine-tuning.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- Left panel: “Input single-cell data”\n  - Shows a 2D embedding/scatter plot of single cells as colored clusters.\n  - Biological source objects are single-cell profiles represented as points.\n- Middle panel: “Cell2Sentence and LLM fine-tuning”\n  - Shows conversion of cells into text-like “cell sentences.”\n  - Example sentences include immune and disease-related cell labels such as “CD4+ T-cell in Human PBMC,” “CD8+ T-cell in Breast Cancer,” “Monocyte in Lupus,” “Hepatocyte in liver tissue,” and “Beta Cell in Type 1 Diabetes.”\n  - Gene symbols appear in sequences, with masked tokens marked as “[MASK].”\n- Right-middle panel: “Generate cell sentences via prompting”\n  - Shows a model interface with:\n    - Model Input: “T-cell in multiple sclerosis”\n    - Model Output: a generated gene/cell sentence containing markers such as CD3E, CD4, IL17A, IFNG, TNF, RORC, TBX21, CCR5, CXCR3, STAT3, FOXP3, GATA3, TGIT, PDCD1, CTLA4, ICOS, CD28, CD25, CD127, IL2, IL7R, IL12RB1, IL23R, CD69, CD44.\n- Far-right panel: “Generated single-cell data”\n  - Shows another 2D embedding/scatter plot with mostly gray cells and a highlighted blue cluster.\n\nTransformations shown:\n- Single-cell data are transformed into textual cell sentences.\n- A language model is fine-tuned on these cell sentences.\n- Prompting the model generates new cell sentences.\n- Generated cell sentences are converted back into generated single-cell data.\n\nMain finding/message:\n- The figure presents a pipeline for generating synthetic or inferred single-cell data from natural-language cell-type/disease prompts using Cell2Sentence representations and an LLM."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Figure 2: Detailed overview of the Cell2Sentence framework. Single-cell gene expression profiles are transformed into cell sentences via expression rank orderering of gene names. Cell sentences may be annotated with biological metadata, such as cell type, tissue, or disease. LLMs are then fine-tuned on the cell sentences. Inference is done by generating cells via autoregressive cell completion, generating cells from text, or generating text from cells. The resulting generated cell sentences can be converted back to gene expression.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic workflow figure for single-cell expression modeling with language-model-style training and downstream generation.\n\nVisible components:\n- Left panel: “Single-cell expression profiles” heatmap with rows labeled Cell 1, Cell 2, Cell 3, …, Cell N and columns labeled genes `g1`, `g2`, `g3`, …, `gm`. A color scale indicates gene expression from Low to High.\n- Transformation: “Rank ordering genes” converts each cell’s expression profile into ordered gene-token sentences.\n- Bottom-left panel: “Cell sentences” lists examples such as `Sentence 1: “g4, g6, …, gj”`, `Sentence 2: “g2, g15, …, g27”`, and an example cell sentence containing marker genes like `RPL1`, `RPL2`, and `RPLX`.\n- Center-bottom panel: “Textual annotation” pairs biological labels with ranked gene sequences, including examples:\n  - `CD4 T-cell`\n  - `T-cell in melanoma`\n  - `Parkinson’s disease`\n  - `Liver tissue`\n- Right-bottom panel: “LLM fine-tuning (autoregressive gene prediction)” shows training inputs where gene sequences end with a green `MASK` token, indicating masked next-token or autoregressive prediction.\n- Top-right panel: “Downstream generative tasks” shows three model interfaces:\n  - “Generating cells from text”: text such as `CD4 T-cell in melanoma` plus `MASK` generates a gene sequence.\n  - “Generating text from cells”: a gene sequence plus `celltype: MASK` generates a label such as `Monocyte`.\n  - “Cell completion”: a partial gene sequence plus `MASK` generates additional genes.\n- Center panel: “Gene expression reconstruction” shows conversion from generated/ranked gene tokens back into a small expression-like bar/heatmap representation.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single cells\n- Genes / gene expression profiles\n- Cell types and biological annotations, including CD4 T-cell, Monocyte, melanoma, Parkinson’s disease, and liver tissue.\n\nModel/interface concept:\n- Gene expression profiles are transformed into ranked gene-token sentences.\n- Textual annotations and cell sentences are used for LLM fine-tuning.\n- The trained model supports inference for generating gene sequences from text, generating annotations from gene sequences, and completing partial cell gene sequences.\n- Generated sequences can be mapped back to reconstructed gene expression."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 5, "caption": "Figure 3: Cell2Sentence transformation can accurately reconstruct cell expression from cell sentences. (A) Expression reconstruction from rank using a linear model. (B) UMAP of ground truth expression (top) versus reconstructed expression from cell sentences (bottom). (C) UMAP of ground truth expression (green) and reconstructed expression from cell sentences overlaid.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure comparing reconstructed and ground-truth single-cell transcriptomic structure.\n\nPanel A: Scatter plot of “Reconstruction from rank” versus “Normalized transcript counts.” Blue points follow a red dashed reference line labeled `y = x`. A statistics box reports Pearson R: 0.934, Spearman R: 0.815, and R²: 0.815.\n\nPanel B: Two UMAP embeddings labeled “Ground Truth” and “Reconstructed.” Both plots show colored cell clusters in UMAP1/UMAP2 space with similar global structure. A legend lists immune cell types, including CD4+ helper T cell, CD8+ memory T cell, CD16+ CD56-bright NK cell, CD16+ CD56-dim NK cell, follicular helper T cell, classical monocyte, effector memory CD4+ α-β T cell, effector memory CD8+ α-β T cell, gamma-delta T cell, macrophage, memory B cell, mucosal invariant T cell, naive B cell, naive thymus-derived CD4+ α-β T cell, naive thymus-derived CD8+ α-β T cell, and regulatory T cell.\n\nPanel C: Overlay UMAP comparison with red points for “Reconstructed” and green points for “Ground Truth.” The plot reports MMD = 0.0020, indicating close distributional agreement between reconstructed and ground-truth embeddings.\n\nBiological source objects: immune cell populations represented as single-cell transcriptomic profiles or embeddings.\n\nTransformations/model interface shown: reconstruction from ranked information or rank-based input to normalized transcript counts and UMAP-space reconstruction, evaluated against ground truth.\n\nVisible finding: reconstructed data closely matches ground truth at both transcript-count correlation level and UMAP distribution level."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 8, "caption": "Figure 4: Plots for cell sentences and expression data. The UMAP plots show that models pretrained with language and fine-tuned with Cell2Sentence achieve quality generated outputs in the sentence space. We see good cell type separation for both generated and ground truth cell sentences in addition to good overlap, showing that our generated sentences model the ground truth distribution well. Plots A, B, and C are UMAP plots of generated cell sentences versus real cell sentences using Levenshtein distance. We computed the Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) [46] statistic with the Python library scMMD [47]. Plots D, E, and F are UMAP plots of generated cell expression vectors and ground truth cell expression vectors. See Table 2 and Table 3 for a quantification of these plots.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure comparing ground truth and generated single-cell data embeddings.\n\nVisible panels:\n- **A**: Overlay scatter plot titled “Sentence embedding (Levenshtein)”. Red points are **Ground Truth**, blue points are **Generated**. Axes include **UMAP 2**; an inset reports **MMD = 0.0007**.\n- **B**: Scatter plot titled **Ground truth** under the sentence embedding section. Points are colored by cell type/class.\n- **C**: Scatter plot titled **Generated** under the sentence embedding section. Same UMAP structure and cell-type color coding as panel B.\n- **D**: Overlay scatter plot for **Gene expression embedding**. Red points are **Ground Truth**, blue points are **Generated**. Axes labeled **UMAP1** and **UMAP2**; inset reports **MMD: 0.0188**.\n- **E**: Scatter plot titled **Ground truth expression**, colored by immune cell categories.\n- **F**: Scatter plot titled **Generated expression**, colored by the same immune cell categories.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell immune-cell populations, including labels such as **CD4+ helper T cell**, **CD8+ memory T cell**, **CD16−/CD56-bright NK cell**, **CD16+/CD56-dim NK cell**, **T follicular helper cell**, **classical monocyte**, **effector memory CD4+ αβ T cell**, **effector memory CD8+ αβ T cell**, **gamma-delta T cell**, **macrophage**, **memory B cell**, **mucosal invariant T cell**, **naive B cell**, **naive thymus-derived CD4+ αβ T cell**, **naive thymus-derived CD8+ αβ T cell**, and **regulatory T cell**.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Data are visualized through **UMAP embeddings**.\n- Two representation spaces are compared: **sentence embedding using Levenshtein** and **gene expression embedding**.\n- The figure compares **ground truth** versus **generated** samples, suggesting evaluation of a generative model or generated single-cell profiles against real/reference data.\n- Similarity is quantified with **MMD** values.\n\nVisible findings:\n- In the sentence embedding space, generated and ground-truth distributions strongly overlap, with low reported **MMD = 0.0007**.\n- In the gene expression embedding space, generated samples broadly reproduce the cluster structure but show a higher discrepancy, with **MMD = 0.0188**."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 10, "caption": "Figure 5: Perplexity curves for finetuned and pretrained models. (A) Estimated model perplexity computed after the training loss. (B) Estimated model perplexity computed on the validation set during training. All models preserve the default GPT-2 context length of 1024 tokens.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nTwo-panel line plot figure labeled **A** and **B**.\n\nPanel **A**: Line chart with x-axis **Steps** from 0 to 10000 and y-axis **Perplexity**. Four training curves are shown: blue and green curves start near high perplexity around 5 and 4.4, then decay toward about 3.1-3.2; orange and red curves start around 2.2 and 1.8, then decay toward about 1.5. No legend is visible in this panel.\n\nPanel **B**: Similar line chart with x-axis **Steps** and y-axis **Perplexity**, including a legend. Curves correspond to:\n- **GPT-2 NL+C2S (117M)**, blue\n- **GPT-2 C2S (93M)**, orange\n- **GPT-2 Medium NL+C2S (345M)**, green\n- **GPT-2 Medium C2S (313M)**, red\n\nVisible finding: perplexity decreases rapidly early in training and then plateaus. The **C2S** models have much lower perplexity than the **NL+C2S** models. The larger **GPT-2 Medium** variants appear slightly lower than the smaller GPT-2 variants within each condition.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy imagery, transformations, or model interface diagrams are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 11, "caption": "Figure 6: Depiction of three types of prompts used during training and generation. From left to right: unconditional cell generation, conditional cell generation (e.g.: with cell type), and autoregressive cell type prediction. For our setup, we generate and prompt with 100 genes.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image contains three side-by-side rounded panels describing model tasks for single-cell gene expression analysis.\n\nPanel 1: “Unconditional cell generation”\n- Shows a model interface with:\n  - Model input: “Rank the first 100 genes by expression level found in a cell.”\n  - Model output: a ranked gene list including IGKV2-14, IGHV4-4, ENSG00000275063.1, MT-CO1, MTRNR2L12, JUN, HSPA1A, IGHG1, HSPD1, IGHG3, DNAJB1, NFKBIA, TPT1, MT-CO2, HSP90AA1, TXNDC5, MT-ATP6, FTL, HSPA8, FKBP11, ZFP36, HSPH1, RPS14, RPS12, RPS27, and others.\n\nPanel 2: “Conditional cell generation”\n- Shows a model interface with:\n  - Model input: “Provide a ranking of the top 100 expressed genes in a CD4 T-cell in PBMC by decreasing levels.”\n  - Model output: a ranked gene list including IGHV3-7, MALAT1, MT-CO2, JCHAIN, IGLV-70, MT-CYB, HSPA6, MT-CO1, TMSB4X, MT-ATP6, MT-ND3, B2M, MT-CO3, MT-ND4L, DNAJB1, UBC, MTRNR2L12, ACTB, RPL41, SSR4, HSP90AA1, HSPA1B, HSPA1A, and others.\n\nPanel 3: “Cell type prediction”\n- Shows a model interface with:\n  - Model input: asks to pinpoint the cell type associated with 100 genes with highest expression, listing genes such as IGHV4-59, IGHG1, HSPA5, HSPA1A, MT-CO1, MT-CO2, MTRNR2L12, HSP90B1, IGKV3-20, RPLP1, MT-CYB, B2M.\n  - Model output: “macrophage.”\n\nBiological source objects: gene expression-ranked cells, PBMC, CD4 T-cell, and predicted macrophage cell type.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: text-prompt inputs are mapped to text outputs for unconditional gene ranking generation, conditional gene ranking generation, and cell type prediction from marker/high-expression genes."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 16, "caption": "Figure 7: Scatter plots showing the log-linear relationship between gene expression and rank across 12 diverse scRNA-seq datasets. The red line shows the fitted linear model, with Pearson's R , Spearman's R , and R 2 quantifying goodness of fit. The high correlation values demonstrate that gene rank encodes expression in a consistent, reversible way across datasets. This enables translating between the text domain of cell sentences and original gene expression.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific plot grid showing scatterplots with fitted regression lines. Each panel compares `Gene Log-Rank` on the x-axis with `Log-Transformed Normalized Gene Expression` on the y-axis.\n\nVisible panels include tissue-group labels such as:\n\n- `Brain Tissue`\n- `Heart, Vascular Tissues`\n- `Lung, Skin/Connective Tissue, Endocrine, Liver, Gastrointestinal Tissue`\n- `Blood Tissue`\n- `Kidney Tissue`\n- `Reproductive Tissue`\n\nEach subplot contains many blue scatter points and a red linear regression line. Legends report fitted linear equations of the form `y = mx + b`, along with `R² Score`, `Pearson's R`, and `Spearman's R`.\n\nVisible findings: across all panels, gene expression decreases as gene log-rank increases, shown by negative regression slopes. Reported correlations are generally strong and negative-trend-associated, with Pearson and Spearman values mostly around `0.7–0.98`, and R² values roughly `0.68–0.95` depending on tissue group. The strongest visible fit appears in one `Blood Tissue` panel with `R² Score: 0.95`, `Pearson's R: 0.98`, and `Spearman's R: 0.98`.\n\nBiological source objects: tissue-specific gene expression measurements grouped by human/animal tissue categories are implied by the labels, but the organism, dataset, and experimental platform are not visible in the image.\n\nTransformations: the y-axis explicitly uses log-transformed normalized gene expression; the x-axis uses gene log-rank. No model interface is shown beyond fitted linear regression summaries."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 17, "caption": "Figure 8: Distributions of Peason's R , Spearman's R and R 2 statistics computed on the performance of expression reconstruction from log-rank using a linear regression across 127 distinct scRNA-seq datasets. Both mean and standard deviation are shown for each score, with the mean represented by a dashed line on the x-axis.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows a three-panel statistical figure with side-by-side histograms summarizing model/dataset performance metrics.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left panel: Histogram of `Pearson's R` with y-axis `Dataset (%)`. Orange bars, dashed vertical reference line around 0.91, annotation `μ = 0.91, σ = 0.04`.\n- Middle panel: Histogram of `Spearman's R` with y-axis `Dataset (%)`. Blue bars, dashed vertical reference line around 0.83, annotation `μ = 0.83, σ = 0.05`.\n- Right panel: Histogram of `R² Score` with y-axis `Dataset (%)`. Green bars, dashed vertical reference line around 0.81, annotation `μ = 0.81, σ = 0.07`.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible. The figure does not show cells, tissues, organisms, genes, proteins, microscopy, sequencing reads, or other biological objects.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- No model architecture, workflow, input/output interface, or transformation pipeline is shown.\n- The figure presents aggregate distributions of evaluation metrics across datasets.\n\nFindings:\n- Most datasets cluster at high performance values.\n- Pearson’s R is concentrated near approximately 0.9–0.95.\n- Spearman’s R is concentrated near approximately 0.8–0.9.\n- R² scores are concentrated near approximately 0.75–0.9.\n- The reported means and standard deviations are Pearson’s R `0.91 ± 0.04`, Spearman’s R `0.83 ± 0.05`, and R² `0.81 ± 0.07`."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 17, "caption": "Figure 9: Density plots of fitted linear regression coefficients relating gene expression to rank across 127 scRNA-seq datasets. The coefficients are used to reconstruct expression from rank, with performance shown in Figure 8. The variability in optimal coefficients highlights the ability to customize reconstruction for diverse datasets.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle-panel quantitative plot showing a 2D density distribution.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis labeled `Intercept`\n- Y-axis labeled `Slope`\n- Colorbar labeled `Density`\n- Density heatmap using a viridis-like scale, ranging from 0.0 to 22.5\n- Overlaid contour lines outlining density levels\n- Two main high-density regions:\n  - Around intercept ~2.15 and slope ~-0.60\n  - Around intercept ~2.55 to 2.65 and slope ~-0.75 to -0.80\n- A low-density background across the rest of the plot\n- Axes span approximately:\n  - Intercept: ~1.55 to ~3.65\n  - Slope: ~-1.4 to ~-0.4\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to visualize estimated or sampled parameter pairs, likely intercept and slope values, as a 2D density/contour plot.\n\nFindings:\n- The distribution is concentrated in two connected clusters with negative slope values, suggesting two modes or grouped parameter estimates."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 18, "caption": "Table 6: Quantification of cell generation accuracy per cell type. PR (Pearson correlation coefficient), SR (Spearman's rank correlation coefficient), and R 2 (coefficient of determination) metrics compare averaged generated cells versus averaged ground truth cells. The high correlation values across multiple cell types demonstrate Cell2Sentence can accurately generate diverse cell types. Pretraining on natural language (NL) further improves performance.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a scientific results table, not a graphical biological figure. It contains model evaluation metrics by immune cell type.\n\nVisible structure:\n- One table with rows labeled by `Cell Type`.\n- Four grouped column sections:\n  - `GPT-2 Small (C2S)`\n  - `GPT-2 Small (NL + C2S)`\n  - `GPT-2 Medium (C2S)`\n  - `GPT-2 Medium (NL + C2S)`\n- Each model section has three metrics:\n  - `PR`\n  - `SR`\n  - `R²`\n- Some values are bolded, likely indicating best or notable performance within rows/metrics.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- The rows are immune or blood cell types, including NK cells, helper T cells, memory T cells, gamma-delta T cells, macrophage, memory B cell, mucosal invariant T cell, naive B cell, thymus-derived T cells, and regulatory T cell.\n- Specific labels include markers such as `CD16-`, `CD56-bright`, `CD16+`, `CD56-dim`, `CD8+`, `α-β`, `CD45RO+`, `CD4+`, and `CD8+`.\n\nModel/interface content:\n- The table compares GPT-2 Small and GPT-2 Medium models.\n- It contrasts `C2S` against `NL + C2S` input/interface conditions.\n- No diagrams of neural architectures, transformations, or biological images are visible.\n\nFindings visible from the table:\n- Reported performance is generally high across many cell types, with PR often around 0.95-0.99 and R² often around 0.90-0.98.\n- `GPT-2 Small (NL + C2S)` and `GPT-2 Medium (NL + C2S)` often show bolded values, especially for PR and R².\n- Some cell types show lower SR or R² values, for example macrophage and regulatory T cell under some model/input settings."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 19, "caption": "Figure 10: Heatmap visualizations comparing differentially expressed genes per cell type between generated and ground truth expression data showing strong correspondence between generated and real cells across a number of cell types. Differentially expressed genes were identified from the top 100 genes in ground truth data.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a two-panel heatmap-style scientific figure comparing cell-type marker/expression patterns.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top panel titled **“Ground truth”**\n- Bottom panel titled **“Generated”**\n\nLabels and axes:\n- Rows are immune cell types, including gamma-delta T cell, mucosal invariant T cell, T follicular helper cell, CD4+ helper T cell, effector memory CD4+ alpha-beta T cell, naive thymus-derived CD4+ alpha-beta T cell, naive thymus-derived CD8+ alpha-beta T cell, effector memory CD8+ alpha-beta T cell, CD8+ alpha-beta memory T cell, CD8+ alpha-beta memory T cell CD45RO+, regulatory T cell, classical monocyte, macrophage, CD16- CD56-bright NK cell, CD16+ CD56-dim NK cell, memory B cell, and naive B cell.\n- Columns are gene/protein marker names shown as dense vertical labels.\n- A color scale at the right maps values approximately from **-1 to 1**, with dark blue/purple low values and yellow high values.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Immune cell populations, likely single-cell or cell-type-level profiles.\n- Marker features include recognizable immune genes/proteins such as CD markers, HLA-related markers, cytokine/receptor-associated genes, and lineage markers.\n\nTransformation/model interface:\n- The figure compares **ground truth** profiles against **generated** profiles, implying a generative model output or synthetic reconstruction of cell-type marker patterns.\n- Both panels use the same cell-type rows and feature columns, enabling direct visual comparison.\n\nVisible findings:\n- The generated heatmap broadly resembles the ground truth in major block patterns across immune cell classes.\n- Distinct high-expression blocks are visible for monocyte/macrophage-associated rows, NK-cell-associated rows, and B-cell-associated rows.\n- Some marker intensities and localized patterns differ between generated and ground truth panels, but the overall structure is preserved."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 20, "caption": "Figure 11: Confusion matrices for KNN classification of cell types using 25 nearest neighbors. Models are (A) GPT-2 Small pretrained, (B) GPT-2 Small fine-tuned, (C) GPT-2 Medium pretrained, and (D) GPT-2 Medium fine-tuned. Fine-tuned models show clearer diagonal patterns, indicating better separation of cell types. This demonstrates improved generation fidelity from natural language pretraining and model scaling.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure showing heatmap-style confusion matrices for cell type classification or separation performance.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Three columns:\n  - “Generated cell separation”\n  - “Ground truth cell separation”\n  - “Generated vs ground truth cell type prediction”\n- Four labeled rows/panels on the left: A), B), C), D)\n- Each panel contains three square heatmaps, for 12 heatmaps total.\n- Axes:\n  - Y-axis label: “Ground Truth Cell Type”\n  - X-axis label: “Predicted Cell Type”\n- Each heatmap uses a viridis-like color scale from dark purple to yellow, with colorbars shown on the right side of the third column.\n- Titles above individual heatmaps report “Average cell-type accuracy,” with visible values including approximately:\n  - A: 32.5846, 60.4518, 23.5059\n  - B: 60.6964, 60.4518, 51.4235\n  - C: 30.2801, 60.4518, 22.1765\n  - D: 57.4788, 60.4518, 45.9294\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Cell types are immune-cell categories, including labels such as CD16+ CD56-bright NK cell, CD16+ CD56-dim NK cell, CD4+ helper T cell, CD8+ memory T cell, CD8+ effector T cell, classical monocyte, effector memory CD4+ T cell, effector memory CD8+ T cell, gamma delta T cell, macrophage, memory B cell, mucosal invariant T cell, naive B cell, naive thymus-derived CD4+ T cell, naive thymus-derived CD8+ T cell, and regulatory T cell.\n\nTransformations / model interfaces:\n- The figure compares generated cell separation, ground-truth cell separation, and generated-vs-ground-truth cell type prediction.\n- The heatmaps appear to encode classification agreement or confusion between predicted and ground-truth cell types across several experimental/model conditions A-D.\n\nVisible finding:\n- Ground-truth separation is consistent across rows with an average cell-type accuracy of about 60.4518.\n- Generated cell separation and generated-vs-ground-truth prediction vary substantially by row, with B and D showing stronger diagonal structure and higher reported accuracies than A and C."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 21, "caption": "Figure 12: Confusion matrices for autoregressive cell type prediction on unseen immune tissue test data. Models pretrained on natural language text before Cell2Sentence training (NL + C2S) significantly outperform models trained only on cell sentences (C2S). This demonstrates the benefits of transfer learning from natural language for predicting cell types from gene sequences.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a 2x2 grid of heatmap panels comparing cell-type classification/confusion patterns for GPT-2 models.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top left: `GPT-2 Small (C2S)`\n- Top right: `GPT-2 Small (NL + C2S)`\n- Bottom left: `GPT-2 Medium (C2S)`\n- Bottom right: `GPT-2 Medium (NL + C2S)`\n\nAxes and labels:\n- The shared y-axis label is `Ground Truth Cell Type`.\n- Both axes list immune or blood cell types, including examples such as classical monocyte, natural killer cell, megakaryocyte, B cell, plasma cell, plasmablast, macrophage, dendritic cell, T-cell subsets, erythroid lineage cell, and others.\n- Heatmap color scale runs approximately from `0` to `100`, with dark purple indicating low values and yellow indicating high values.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Human immune/hematopoietic cell types are the biological categories being compared.\n- Labels include specific lymphoid, myeloid, dendritic, erythroid, and progenitor-like cell populations.\n\nTransformations / model interfaces:\n- Panels compare two model sizes, `GPT-2 Small` and `GPT-2 Medium`.\n- Each model is shown under two input/interface conditions: `C2S` and `NL + C2S`.\n- The figure appears to visualize prediction distribution or confusion matrices between ground-truth cell types and predicted cell-type labels.\n\nFindings visible from the image:\n- The `NL + C2S` panels show a strong bright diagonal, indicating much better alignment between ground-truth and predicted labels.\n- The `C2S`-only panels show sparse vertical bands and off-diagonal activations, suggesting predictions collapse toward a limited subset of labels rather than matching each ground-truth class.\n- GPT-2 Small and GPT-2 Medium show broadly similar qualitative behavior: adding `NL` to `C2S` visibly improves diagonal structure."]}, {"figure_index": 14, "page_no": 22, "caption": "Figure 13: UMAP plots comparing cell embeddings from C2S, scVI, and ground truth data. The Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) statistic quantifies the difference in distributions. The lower MMDvalue for C2S indicates that our generated cells better match the real data distribution than the cells generated with scVI.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single UMAP scatter plot comparing generated and real/generated cell populations.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Main axis label: `UMAP1` on the x-axis; y-axis label is not visible.\n- Legend in the upper right:\n  - Blue: `C2S generated`\n  - Red: `Ground Truth`\n  - Green: `scVI generated cells`\n- Text box in the upper left:\n  - `MMD C2S: 0.0188`\n  - `MMD scVI: 0.3253`\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- Points represent cells or cell embeddings, likely single-cell data.\n- Three cell distributions are compared: C2S-generated cells, ground-truth cells, and scVI-generated cells.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Data are visualized after dimensionality reduction with UMAP.\n- The figure compares model-generated cell distributions against ground truth using MMD scores.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Blue C2S-generated points overlap substantially with red ground-truth points across multiple clusters.\n- Green scVI-generated cells form largely separate clusters, especially a large isolated group on the lower left.\n- The reported MMD is much lower for C2S (`0.0188`) than for scVI (`0.3253`), indicating closer agreement between C2S-generated and ground-truth distributions in this visualization."]}, {"figure_index": 15, "page_no": 22, "caption": "Figure 14: UMAP visualization colored by cell type, comparing C2S, scVI, and ground truth data. Both C2S and scVI generate distinct cell types, but only C2S cells strongly align with ground truth cells of the matching type. This shows that the C2S framework can generate specific cell types that closely mimic real cells, outperforming scVI.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows a three-panel scientific scatter plot comparing UMAP embeddings.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left: “Ground truth”\n- Middle: “Cell2Sentence generated”\n- Right: “scVI generated”\n\nAxes and labels:\n- Each panel has x-axis labeled “UMAP1” and y-axis labeled “UMAP2”.\n- Points are colored by immune cell type.\n- A shared legend appears below the panels.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell populations represented as colored points.\n- Legend includes immune cell types such as macrophage, CD4+ helper T cell, memory B cell, naive B cell, gamma-delta T cell, regulatory T cell, classical monocyte, naive thymus-derived CD4+ α-β T cell, naive thymus-derived CD8+ α-β T cell, effector memory CD4+ α-β T cell, CD8+ α-β memory T cell, CD16−/CD56-bright NK cell, CD16+/CD56-dim NK cell, mucosal invariant T cell, effector memory CD8+ α-β T cell, CD8+ α-β memory T cell CD45RO+, and follicular helper T cell.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- UMAP dimensionality reduction is used to visualize cell embeddings.\n- The figure compares real/ground-truth cell distributions against generated outputs from two models: Cell2Sentence and scVI.\n\nVisible findings:\n- The Cell2Sentence generated panel visually resembles the ground truth structure more closely, preserving several separated clusters and broad spatial organization.\n- The scVI generated panel shows a large dense mixed cluster on the left, sparse dispersed points in the upper/right regions, and smaller separated clusters near the lower-right, appearing less similar to the ground truth distribution."]}]

